ting to important cross-national differences in how political polarization manifests and impacts health behaviors.", "quotes": [{"text": "This relationship was notably weaker in Canada, pointing to important cross-national differences in how political polarization manifests and impacts health behaviors.", "page": 1}]}], "scope_and_limits": [{"claim": "The analysis is based on Twitter data, which reflects a vocal subset of politically engaged users rather than the broader public.", "quotes": [{"text": "Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "The analysis is restricted to English-language tweets, which in Canada means perspectives of francophone-only users are underrepresented, potentially biasing results particularly for Quebec.", "quotes": [{"text": "we restricted our analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "The correlations between polarization and public health outcomes should be understood as suggestive rather than causative, due to temporal mismatches and the inherently correlational nature of the analysis.", "quotes": [{"text": "while several of our analyses rely on correlations, these results do not imply causation", "page": 25}]}], "theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The authors position their text-based embedding approach as an accessible and scalable alternative to network-based methods, which have become less feasible due to platform data restrictions, while acknowledging the methodological advantages of embedding-based models over traditional lexicon-based strategies.", "quotes": [{"text": "Consequently, our study adopts a strictly text-based method, which remains both accessible and scalable for analyzing large-scale digital discourse.", "page": 3}, {"text": "Recent computational approaches increasingly draw on text embedding methods to study polarization, using contextualized representations to detect partisan divides across topics and communities", "page": 3}]}], "interlocutor_claim": [{"claim": "The paper attributes to Owen et al. [94] the finding of reduced negative sentiment in Canadian vaccine-related tweets between January and December 2020, and disputes this by showing no statistically significant correlation between polarization and conspiracy-related tweet volume in Canada.", "quotes": [{"text": "This contrasts with [94], who found reduced negative sentiment in Canadian vaccine-related tweets between January and December 2020.", "page": 24}]}}]}
